/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The chain is antenna, RF band-pass filter (preselector), low-noise amplifier (LNA), image filter, mixer with a local oscillator (LO), IF filter, IF amplifier and demodulator.
   - fRF is the wanted radio frequency, fLO the local-oscillator frequency and fIF the intermediate frequency, all in Hz.
   - A second input frequency, the image, gives the same IF from the other side of the LO.
   - A single tuned circuit of loaded quality factor Q, tuned to fs, attenuates the image by the image rejection ratio
   - α is a voltage ratio (20 log₁₀α in dB); fs is the signal frequency and fsi the image frequency.
   - This is the IF trade-off: a high IF puts the image far away but makes a sharp channel filter hard; a low IF does the reverse.
   - A direct-conversion receiver sets fLO = fRF, so the IF is zero.
   - With no image or IF filter it suits a single-chip radio.
   - In the Hartley architecture the RF signal drives two mixers with LO signals at cos ωLOt and sin ωLOt.
   - The image-reject ratio (IRR) is the conversion gain for the wanted signal divided by the conversion gain for the image.
*/
