/* book Worked example: Q: A 900 MHz receiver has a 45 MHz IF and a high-side LO. (a) Find fLO and the image frequency. (b) The preselector is one tuned circuit with loaded Q = 50. Find its image rejection. (c) A −20 dBm signal sits on the image while the wanted signal is −80 dBm. After the preselector, how strong is the image compared with the wanted signal? (d) How many such tuned stages are needed to bring the image 20 dB below the wanted signal?
   1. fLO = 900 + 45 = 945 MHz; fsi = 945 + 45 = 990 MHz (= 900 + 2 × 45).
   2. ρ = 990/900 − 900/990 = 1.1000 − 0.9091 = 0.1909.
   3. Qρ = 50 × 0.1909 = 9.545; α = √(1 + 9.545²) = √92.11 = 9.60, that is 20 log₁₀ 9.60 = 19.6 dB.
   4. Image after the preselector: −20 − 19.6 = −39.6 dBm, still 40.4 dB above the −80 dBm wanted signal.
   5. Target: image at −80 − 20 = −100 dBm, so 80 dB of rejection is needed. Each stage gives 19.6 dB: 80/19.6 = 4.1, so five tuned stages (98.2 dB), or a sharper SAW image filter.
   Answer: fLO = 945 MHz, image = 990 MHz, one Q = 50 stage rejects it by 19.6 dB, leaving it 40.4 dB above the wanted signal; about 80 dB is needed, five such stages or a dedicated image filter. */
