/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Digital baseband produces two sample streams, I and Q.
   - Direct up-conversion has two weaknesses: LO feedthrough leaves a spur at fLO in mid-channel, and the large PA output at the VCO's own frequency can couple back and pull the VCO (LO pulling).
   - Two-step up-conversion first mixes baseband to a fixed IF, filters it, then mixes to RF with a second LO.
   - EIRP (effective isotropic radiated power) in dBm; PPA is the PA output in dBm; Lfeed is the total loss in dB of the harmonic filter, duplexer or switch and cable between the PA and the antenna; Gant is the antenna gain in dBi.
   - A spectral mask is a regulatory or standard limit on transmitted power against frequency offset from the carrier, given as absolute power in a measurement bandwidth.
   - Modulated signals have a peak-to-average power ratio (PAPR), so the average output must be backed off from compression by about the PAPR: an 8 dB PAPR signal at 25 dBm average has 33 dBm peaks.
   - In frequency-division duplex (FDD) the transmitter and receiver work at the same time on two frequencies, joined to one antenna by a duplexer: two band-pass filters, one for each band.
   - In time-division duplex (TDD) the two directions take turns on one frequency, so a switch replaces the duplexer.
*/
