/* book Worked example: Q: In a direct-conversion transmitter the I/Q modulator gives −5 dBm, the driver has 10 dB gain and the PA 20 dB gain with P1dB,out = 33 dBm. The harmonic filter loses 0.5 dB and the duplexer 1.5 dB; the antenna gain is 3 dBi. The signal has PAPR = 8 dB and the PA achieves ACLR = 45 dB. Find the PA output, the antenna-port power, the EIRP in dBm and watts, the peak power at the PA output and the power leaked into the adjacent channel at the antenna port.
   1. PPA = −5 + 10 + 20 = 25 dBm (316 mW average).
   2. Lfeed = 0.5 + 1.5 = 2 dB, so Pant = 25 − 2 = 23 dBm (200 mW).
   3. EIRP = 23 + 3 = 26 dBm = 102.6 mW = 398 mW, about 0.40 W. The same from PPA − Lfeed + Gant = 25 − 2 + 3.
   4. Peak PA output = 25 + 8 = 33 dBm, exactly P1dB,out: the peaks reach 1 dB of compression, so this is the most average power the PA can give without linearisation.
   5. Adjacent-channel power at the antenna port = 23 − 45 = −22 dBm (6.3 µW).
   Answer: PPA = 25 dBm, Pant = 23 dBm, EIRP = 26 dBm (0.40 W), peaks at 33 dBm (right at P1dB,out), adjacent-channel leakage −22 dBm. */
