/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The LNA's noise figure adds almost directly to the receiver's, because the first stage dominates the Friis sum, so 1 dB saved here is about 1 dB of sensitivity.
   - F and Fmin are linear noise factors (convert to dB only at the end, NF = 10 log₁₀F); Rn in Ω; Z₀ is the reference impedance, usually 50 Ω; Γs and Γopt are complex reflection coefficients.
   - On a Smith chart points of equal F form circles around Γopt.
   - The standard narrowband CMOS LNA is a common-source MOSFET with an inductor Ls in its source and an inductor Lg in series with its gate.
   - gm is the transconductance in S; Cgs the gate-source capacitance in F; ωT = gm/Cgs is the transit frequency in rad/s (the datasheet value fT = ωT/2π is in Hz).
   - The real part ωTLs is made by feedback through Ls, not by a resistor, so it adds no thermal noise of its own.
   - A cascode transistor stacked on top (common-gate) cuts the Miller feedback from drain to gate, so the input match does not depend on the load and reverse isolation is high.
   - An LNA needs just enough gain, typically 12 to 20 dB: enough to make the mixer's term (F₂ − 1)/G₁ small, not so much that the stages after it compress first; cascaded IIP3 falls as G₁ rises.
   - The LNA must not oscillate with any passive source or load.
   - K > 1 together with |Δ| < 1 means unconditionally stable at that frequency; check it at every frequency where the device has gain.
*/
