/* book Worked example: Q: Design the input of an inductively degenerated CMOS LNA for 2.4 GHz and a 50 Ω source. The transistor has gm = 60 mS and Cgs = 300 fF. Find ωT, fT, Ls, Lg, Qin, the effective transconductance and the input return loss at 2.3 and 2.5 GHz.
   1. ωT = gm/Cgs = 0.06/(300 × 10⁻¹⁵) = 2.0 × 10¹¹ rad/s; fT = ωT/2π = 31.8 GHz.
   2. Ls = 50/ωT = 50/(2 × 10¹¹) = 0.25 nH (a bond wire or a short on-chip line).
   3. ω₀ = 2π × 2.4 × 10⁹ = 1.508 × 10¹⁰ rad/s; Lg + Ls = 1/(ω₀²Cgs) = 1/((1.508 × 10¹⁰)² × 300 × 10⁻¹⁵) = 14.66 nH.
   4. Lg = 14.66 − 0.25 = 14.41 nH, too large for a good on-chip spiral, so it is usually an off-chip inductor or part of the package.
   5. At 2.4 GHz, 1/(ω₀Cgs) = 221 Ω; Qin = 221/(50 + 50) = 2.21. Effective transconductance = gmQin = 60 × 2.21 = 132.6 mS.
   6. At 2.3 GHz: Zin = 50 − j18.8 Ω, |Γ| = 0.185, return loss 14.7 dB. At 2.5 GHz: Zin = 50 + j18.1 Ω, |Γ| = 0.178, return loss 15.0 dB. The whole 2.4 GHz band is matched better than 10 dB.
   Answer: ωT = 2 × 10¹¹ rad/s (fT = 31.8 GHz), Ls = 0.25 nH, Lg = 14.41 nH, Qin = 2.21, effective gm = 132.6 mS, return loss about 15 dB at ±100 MHz. */
