/* book exam traps: Putting Fmin in dB into the noise-parameter formula. The formula works in linear factors; 1.0 dB is Fmin = 1.259. | Assuming Γopt = Γin*. The lowest-noise source and the matched source are different points, so lowest NF does not mean lowest reflection. | Taking ωT as fT. Rin = ωTLs uses rad/s; using fT in Hz makes Ls 2π times too large. | Adding a 50 Ω resistor at the gate to get a match. It matches, but its own noise sets NF above 3 dB; degeneration gives the real part without noise. | Calling an amplifier stable because K > 1. Unconditional stability needs |Δ| < 1 as well, at every frequency with gain. */
